Hall of Amrita Precepts (甘露戒堂)
Kong Meng San Phor Kark See Monastery

Preliminary observation
The institutional source resolves identifications and reveals that this hall is functionally specialized in a way previously undocumented in this corpus. Completed in 1982, the Hall of Amrita Precepts is the ordination hall — the dedicated sacred space where novice monastics receive their precepts and are admitted to the Sangha. This is a profoundly significant institutional function: every monk at Kong Meng San Phor Kark See has been inducted into monastic life within this hall, and its iconographic programme is explicitly designed to support the precept-taking ritual that constitutes the foundation of monastic identity.
The hall's name derives from a soteriological metaphor: 甘露 (gānlù, amṛta, divine nectar) is the heavenly substance that relieves hunger and thirst. The precepts (śīla) function analogously — they nourish and sustain the practitioner's dharmakāya (法身慧命, "Dharma-body and wisdom-life"). Through diligent observance of the precepts, the practitioner purifies bodily, verbal, and mental karma, attains stability of mind and body, and is led toward liberation from saṃsāra. The hall is therefore not merely a space where ordination happens to occur — it is a hall whose entire iconographic programme theologizes the act of precept-taking as the soteriological foundation of monastic life.
The architectural form is itself doctrinally significant: an octagonal lotus platform symbolizing the Noble Eightfold Path, with the principal Buddha enthroned at its centre on a thousand-petalled lotus and the eight corners guarded by Vajrakumāras (Vajra Guardian Youths). Every architectural decision — the eight-sided platform, the thousand petals, the eight guardians — encodes a specific element of Buddhist doctrine. The hall is a doctrinal cosmogram before it is anything else.

Step 1 — ICON descriptions
ICON₁ — Locana Buddha (盧舍那佛, Vairocana / Saṃbhogakāya) (Central, enthroned on thousand-petalled lotus)
POSITION    : central, axial, elevated
SCALE       : dominant
ORIENTATION : front-facing
FUNCTION    : soteriological, devotional, cosmological, pedagogical
The principal deity is Locana Buddha (盧舍那佛, Lúshènà Fó) — also known as Vairocana in some Chinese traditions, though the institutional source specifies Locana. This is a critically important identification. Locana represents the Saṃbhogakāya (報身, "Body of Fruition" or "Bliss Body") — the manifestation of the Buddha's perfected wisdom attained through complete cultivation. He is not Śākyamuni in his historical manifestation (Nirmāṇakāya), nor Vairocana in his cosmic absolute form (Dharmakāya), but the intermediate body of fruition: Buddhahood as the perfected result of the bodhisattva path. This identification is precisely appropriate to a precept hall: the precepts are the foundation of cultivation that culminates in the Saṃbhogakāya manifestation, and to take precepts under Locana's gaze is to orient oneself toward the fruition that diligent observance produces.
The thousand-petalled lotus throne is itself a doctrinally charged iconographic element. In the Brahmajāla Sūtra (梵網經), the foundational Mahāyāna text on bodhisattva precepts, Locana is described as seated on a thousand-petalled lotus, with each petal containing a world in which a Śākyamuni Buddha teaches and transmits the same precepts. The lotus throne therefore encodes not just a sacred seat but the cosmological infrastructure of bodhisattva-precept transmission across all worlds.

ICON₂ — Avalokiteśvara (觀世音菩薩) — Great Compassion ICON₃ — Mañjuśrī (文殊菩薩) — Great Wisdom ICON₄ — Kṣitigarbha (地藏菩薩) — Great Vow ICON₅ — Samantabhadra (普賢菩薩) — Great Practice
ICON — each Bodhisattva
POSITION    : peripheral, recessed (along the octagonal walls)
SCALE       : subordinate (relative to Locana)
ORIENTATION : front-facing or oriented toward the central Buddha
FUNCTION    : pedagogical, soteriological, devotional
This is the Four Great Bodhisattvas (四大菩薩) configuration in its complete form — the first time this canonical grouping appears in the corpus with all four members present. The previous Three Great Bodhisattvas configuration at the Hall of Great Compassion (Avalokiteśvara, Mañjuśrī, Samantabhadra) is here completed by the addition of Kṣitigarbha. Each bodhisattva carries a single foundational virtue:
Avalokiteśvara  →  大悲  (mahā-karuṇā, Great Compassion)
Mañjuśrī        →  大智  (mahā-prajñā, Great Wisdom)
Kṣitigarbha     →  大願  (mahā-praṇidhāna, Great Vow)
Samantabhadra   →  大行  (mahā-caryā, Great Practice)
These four virtues together constitute the complete spiritual orientation required of the monastic taking precepts. To enter monastic life is to commit to compassion, wisdom, vow, and practice as the four irreducible dimensions of the path. The hall's configuration ensures that the ordinand stands surrounded by the four bodhisattva exemplars during the precept ritual itself — each virtue made spatially present, each commitment iconographically witnessed.
The sacred mountains compression is now complete. Where the Hall of Great Compassion gathered three of the Four Sacred Mountains (Putuo, Wutai, Emei), this hall gathers all four:
Avalokiteśvara  →  Mount Putuo (普陀山)    -- East China Sea
Mañjuśrī        →  Mount Wutai (五台山)    -- Shanxi
Kṣitigarbha     →  Mount Jiuhua (九華山)   -- Anhui
Samantabhadra   →  Mount Emei (峨眉山)     -- Sichuan
The Hall of Amrita Precepts is therefore a complete pilgrimage cosmogram — every Sacred Mountain spatially present as the ordinand takes their vows.

ICON₆–ICON₁₃ — Eight Vajrakumāras (八大金剛) (Eight corners of octagonal platform)
POSITION    : peripheral, paired-lateral × 4 corners
SCALE       : subordinate
ORIENTATION : outward-facing
FUNCTION    : apotropaic, protective
The eight wrathful guardian figures positioned at the corners of the octagonal platform — visible in the photograph as muscular, dynamically-posed marble figures — are Vajrakumāras (金剛, vajra-bearing youths). The institutional explanation is theologically precise: their fierce appearance does not signify ordinary aggression but resolute strength in upholding the Noble Eightfold Path. They are wrathful only toward kleśas (defilements) — the inner enemies that bind sentient beings in suffering. Their apotropaic function is therefore directed inward (against the practitioner's own defilements) rather than only outward (against external threat). They protect the precept-taker from the obstacles to precept-keeping.
Their number — eight — corresponds directly to the eight petals of the Noble Eightfold Path that the platform itself symbolizes. Each guardian stands at a corner of the platform, marking and defending one element of the path:
Right View (正見)         Right Intention (正思惟)
Right Speech (正語)        Right Action (正業)
Right Livelihood (正命)    Right Effort (正精進)
Right Mindfulness (正念)   Right Concentration (正定)

Step 2 — Architectural cosmogram
The hall's architecture is itself a doctrinal statement that must be encoded through frames and structural relations:
ICON — Octagonal Lotus Platform
ARCHITECTURAL FUNCTION : cosmological
  manifests Noble Eightfold Path through eight-sided form
  encodes integration of body and mind through the
  practitioner's ascent of the path

ICON — Thousand-Petalled Lotus Throne
ARCHITECTURAL FUNCTION : cosmological
  manifests the Brahmajāla cosmos in which Locana
  presides over infinite worlds, each containing
  a Śākyamuni who transmits the same precepts
The ceiling decoration visible in the photograph — heavenly beings (天人) offering alms, music, and precious items — extends this cosmological programme to the upper register. The walls and ceiling depict what the institutional source describes as 天人供養圖, the heavenly assembly making offerings to the Buddha. The architectural envelope of the hall therefore depicts the cosmic audience of devotees that surrounds Locana's precept-transmission across all worlds.

