Step 4 — Grammar note: the quadruple production
This hall introduces the corpus's first quadruple configuration — four bodhisattvas organized around a central Buddha. The grammar's existing productions handled dyadic and triadic ensembles, but the Four Great Bodhisattvas configuration requires explicit accommodation of four-around-one:
ENSEMBLE → [ICON₁  {centers}  ICON₂  ICON₃  ICON₄  ICON₅]
           -- quadruple production with central anchor;
           -- the four peripheral figures together constitute
              a complete cardinal-virtue programme
The quadruple production differs from the triadic in an important respect: the four peripheral figures complete a closed virtue system. The triadic configuration of compassion-wisdom-practice could be theologically extended (some traditions add vow); the quadruple of compassion-wisdom-vow-practice is the complete system. There is no fifth virtue waiting to be added. The configuration is doctrinally exhaustive.
The grammar should also note that the eight Vajrakumāras introduce an octuple guardian configuration — eight protective figures rather than the more common pair (Sangharama and Skanda). The octuple form is generated by the Eightfold Path symbolism rather than by canonical guardianship pairing. RFIG accommodates this through the brackets relation applied to a multi-icon set rather than a pair, and the grammar should note that brackets — which previously assumed paired threshold figures — can extend to higher-order numerical configurations when the doctrinal logic requires.

Step 5 — The ordination ritual as the hall's primary operation
Unlike most halls in this corpus, which are devotional spaces visited by lay devotees, the Hall of Amrita Precepts is functionally specialized for a single ritual operation: the precept-taking ceremony for monastic ordinands. The ICON descriptions and ENSEMBLE statements above describe the static iconographic programme, but the hall's analytical fullness only emerges when this static programme is read against the dynamic ritual that activates it.
When an ordinand takes precepts, they kneel at the centre of the octagonal platform, before Locana on the thousand-petalled lotus, surrounded by:
- The four bodhisattva exemplars whose virtues they commit to embody
- The eight Vajrakumāras whose protection extends specifically to
  defending the ordinand against their own defilements
- The walls and ceiling depicting the heavenly assembly that
  witnesses every Buddhist precept-taking across all worlds
The ritual operation transforms the hall from a static cosmogram into an activated soteriological event. The ordinand becomes the central agent within the cosmogram: they take precepts in the presence of the Saṃbhogakāya Buddha, witnessed by the heavenly assembly, surrounded by the four bodhisattva virtues they commit to, and protected by the eightfold guardianship of the path itself.
In RFIG terms, this introduces a relation type the grammar has not yet been asked to handle: a relation between iconography and ritual subject, where the ordinand becomes a momentary occupant of the cosmogram's central position during the precept ceremony.
[ORDINAND_AT_PRECEPT_RITUAL  centers  HALL_ENSEMBLE]
  [scope: shrine, ritual-specific]
  -- the iconographic configuration is fully activated
     only when an ordinand occupies the platform's centre
     during the precept-taking ceremony
This is the temporality parameter of the coordinates relation extended into a new domain: the hall's iconographic meaning is partly ritual-specific. Outside the precept ceremony, the cosmogram remains visually present but unactivated. During the ceremony, the cosmogram comes alive around the ordinand, and the ordinand becomes the figure for whom the entire iconographic programme exists.
